WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.938
- [Jordan] These are government bonds.

00:00:01.938 --> 00:00:02.771
(transition thudding)

00:00:02.771 --> 00:00:05.850
Even if you know nothing about
them, you might have heard.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.640
- Treasuries have proven
always to be a reliable,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.840
safe haven investment.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.280
- Recession fears have caused investors

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.830
to flee to safe investments
like Treasury Bonds.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.370
- U.S. Treasury Bonds are
a pretty safe investment.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.720
- Nothing can touch them

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.520
- [Jordan] But there's a caveat.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.770
Not all bonds are the same.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.010
Some let you take a bit of
a gamble by selling them.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.247
And if you do, like
Silicon Valley Bank had to,

00:00:29.247 --> 00:00:31.200
you introduce the one thing

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.193
that everyone says bonds don't have, risk.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:40.200
We break down how the fail
safe investment can fail.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.077
And why despite that,
bonds are still so safe.

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.072
(gentle music)

00:00:47.072 --> 00:00:48.300
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.190
Bonds are IOUs.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.800
- The government gets a
lot of money from taxes

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.477
but it spends more money.

00:00:54.477 --> 00:00:57.180
So it has to borrow money.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.620
- [Jordan] And that's where bonds

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.500
aka Treasury securities come in.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.210
You give the government money

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.610
and it promises to give
it back at a later date

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.620
with some interest.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.100
And the government takes
this promise very seriously.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.430
This makes bonds an ideal
investment if safety,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.930
rather than a large return,
is an investor's top priority.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.800
Bonds tend to be a popular
investment tool for retirees,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.890
investors looking to add some
cushion to their portfolio,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.250
or parents looking to start
their kids' college fund.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.650
And the bond market, as we know it today,

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.512
can be traced all the way
back to the First World War.

00:01:34.512 --> 00:01:35.730
(tape whirring)

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.280
- [Narrator] From a
pistol shot at Sarajevo,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.370
the first the great
modern world wars explode.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.780
- [Jordan] In 1917,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.450
the U.S. government
released Liberty Loans,

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.180
a type of bond meant to
garner public support

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.070
and funds for the war.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.750
People could cash them in
typically 10 to 30 years later.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.593
Ultimately, Liberty Bonds
raised more than $17 billion.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.980
That's 2/3 of the funds America raised

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.780
for the First World War.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.840
It marked the birth of
the modern Treasury market

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.579
and introduced investing
to many Americans.

00:02:09.579 --> 00:02:13.200
♪ We've got another bond to buy. ♪

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.620
- The government issued more
war bonds during World War II.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.770
After the war, they were
converted to savings bonds.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.210
- [Narrator] It's your future.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.370
Build for it, save for it.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.420
Buy shares in it.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.300
- [Jordan] We still have
savings bonds today.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.560
Like war bonds,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.380
they can only be bought
directly from the government,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.940
and you can't sell them.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.528
Though, you can transfer
them to people as gifts.

00:02:36.528 --> 00:02:39.152
- (indistinct) gift, Merry Christmas.

00:02:39.152 --> 00:02:39.985
- Thank you.

00:02:39.985 --> 00:02:41.910
- [Jordan] The only thing
that could prevent you

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.790
from getting your money when
your savings bonds mature

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.430
is if the Treasury defaulted.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.500
Okay, it wouldn't look like that,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.630
but the government defaulting on its debts

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.130
would be catastrophic.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.370
However, it's incredibly
unlikely to happen.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.380
- The fact that everybody thinks

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.650
that treasuries are safe
helps make them safe.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.860
Investors around the world
just pile into treasuries.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.870
That makes it very, very easy

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.960
for the U.S. government
to raise more money

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.460
in the bond market.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.590
- [Jordan] This perpetuates a cycle.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.570
People buy bonds from the government.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.250
The government spends the money.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.170
It issues new bonds.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.360
And then it uses some
of the new bond money

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.250
to pay everyone back.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.083
Plus.

00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:26.370
- The US government can always raise taxes

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.240
or cut spending in order to
pay back its bond holders.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.460
- [Jordan] So the way to
introduce risk to bonds

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.523
really comes from, well, you.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.990
Not all bonds function like savings bonds.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:44.460
Take these securities, Treasury
Bills, Notes, and Bonds

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.560
you can buy in the primary market,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.830
meaning you get them new
in a government auction.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.810
Or the secondary market,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.030
meaning you buy them from another seller.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.850
And unlike savings bonds,
you can sell these bonds

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.360
to someone else in the secondary
market before they mature.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.330
But if you do, you introduce risk.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.060
So why take a perfectly safe investment

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.190
and make it less safe?

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.810
- The benefit of the secondary market

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.470
is that if you need the money,
you can always sell the bond.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:17.490
And you don't have to wait 10
years until the bond matures.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.110
- [Jordan] But if you sell a bond,

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:22.650
you're not guaranteed to get
the face value you paid for it.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.830
That's because a bond's
value in this market

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.510
is always changing.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:28.410
In that way,

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:31.770
the bond market looks a
bit like the stock market.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.620
To understand how this risk
works in the real world,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.660
let's look at Silicon Valley Bank

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.570
- Silicon Valley Bank was
getting a lot of money in

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.670
from the tech sector,

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.130
and it needed a place to put that money.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.140
- [Jordan] So it bought
billions of dollars

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.870
of medium to longer term notes and bonds

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.390
for the same reason anyone buys them,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.420
they're a safe place to
park money for a while.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.270
However, at the time the
bank bought these bonds,

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.760
the interest rate on the bonds was low.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.150
If the bank had been able to
hold its bonds to maturity,

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.640
then the interest rate
wouldn't have mattered.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.850
The bank would've cashed in
their billions, plus interest,

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.860
and that would've been that.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.470
Instead, a few things went wrong.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.300
to ease rising inflation,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.820
the Treasury increased interest rates.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.120
And when interest rates
rise, bond prices fall.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.940
This left a roughly 17 billion gap

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.040
between what the bank paid for the bonds

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.690
and what their value was,

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.180
putting the bank in a bad position.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.910
To get more cash and buy newer bonds,

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.580
Silicon Valley Bank had to
sell some of their bonds

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.980
at a loss in the secondary market.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.200
This helped trigger a run on the bank

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.630
and led to its collapse.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.970
Now, this is an extreme example

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.310
of what can go wrong in the bond market.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.980
- What happened to Silicon
Valley Bank is not the norm.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.890
Most people aren't forced to sell

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.220
billions of dollars of
bonds at a big loss.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.410
If you have to sell them early,

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.960
you probably won't see a big loss.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.160
And you could even see a gain

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.230
if interest rates are falling.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.050
- Ultimately, there are
still no safer investments

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:08.400
than government bonds.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.880
- Stock prices can go
down like 4% in a day.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.827
On a day-to-day basis,

00:06:13.827 --> 00:06:16.770
you don't have that risk if
you're buying and selling bonds

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.420
that you do have in the stock market.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.740
- [Jordan] That's why they say.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.180
- Nothing can touch them.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.133
- [Jordan] Well, almost nothing.

